      <p class="eyebrow">NEXTBODY MCP</p>
      <h1>How NEXTBODY MCP connects health data to ChatGPT and Claude</h1>
      <p class="meta">
        <time datetime="2026-10-26">26 October 2026</time>
        <a href="{% if blogs.news.handle != blank %}{{ blogs.news.url }}{% elsif pages.journal.url != blank %}{{ pages.journal.url }}{% else %}{{ routes.all_products_collection_url }}?view=journal{% endif %}">All notes</a>
      </p>
      <p class="lede">A technical note on what the product site already says about MCP: who can connect, what they can read, and what they must not be used for.</p>
    </header>

    <p>HOOP is a screenless smart health band (8mm thin, 10g without the strap). The NEXTBODY App is where you read heart rate, sleep staging, training load and a 14-metric Body Scan. MCP is the optional bridge from that account to a third-party assistant.</p>

    <h2>What MCP does</h2>
    <p>NEXTBODY MCP is not a public dump of everyone&#x27;s data. Connecting a third-party AI assistant requires your own sign-in and authorisation. The assistant can only read data under your account.</p>
    <p>Supported assistants named on the site today are ChatGPT, Claude, Grok Bot and Muse. Names are used to describe compatibility. They do not imply a partnership or endorsement, and the list may change with what the App actually supports.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Once you authorise it, the assistant can read your own body data from the NEXTBODY App.</li>
      <li>It cannot read another person&#x27;s account.</li>
      <li>If you do not sign in and authorise the connection, MCP does not share that data.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Meal logging stays off until you turn it on</h2>
    <p>Meal logging is off by default. After you turn it on, you can log a meal in one sentence. The assistant confirms the food, portion and time with you before saving. Calories, protein, carbs and fat are estimated from that description — not weighed or lab-tested — and daily targets are general guidance, not a nutrition prescription.</p>

    <h2>What sits behind the connection</h2>
    <p>MCP reads the same wellness record the App already shows. The band is screenless; the App is the display. Claims below are the ones already published on nextbody.ai.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Readiness (0–100) starts from last night&#x27;s sleep and compares night HRV and resting heart rate with your personal baseline. It is an algorithm estimate for reference only.</li>
      <li>Sleep score is out of 100, weighted across seven factors by HOOP&#x27;s own rules. It is not a clinical scale.</li>
      <li>Training load (0–21) tallies heart-rate zones and activity every 5 minutes and does not replace a coach.</li>
      <li>Body Scan uses bioimpedance plus the height, weight, age and sex in the App to estimate 14 body-composition metrics. Results are estimates, not a medical diagnosis. Do not use Body Scan with a pacemaker or other implanted electronic medical device.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Limits, privacy and tone</h2>
    <p>Once data is sent to a third-party AI service, that service&#x27;s privacy policy applies, so decide for yourself before connecting. The assistant&#x27;s analysis and advice are for reference only — not medical, nutrition or training prescriptions. HOOP and the NEXTBODY App are not medical devices and are not intended to diagnose, treat, cure or prevent any disease. If you feel unwell, see a doctor.</p>

